In this turn, the solver requested three demonstrations consecutively without first integrating each returned pair, despite the compact-grid task's representation being unresolved. The three input/output pairs varied in dimensions, palettes, and compact output shapes (23×23→5×6, 26×22→4×3, 27×29→6×4). The test itself has a 14×28 lattice with two horizontal and three vertical panel intervals; separator intersections include color 1 in the upper-left and color 4 across the right-side intersections. No common exact mapping was established from the three demos.

A speculative 4×4 grid of repeated marker colors was then submitted and rejected. This falsifies that candidate in full; do not tweak its colors or dimensions without new evidence. The key process correction is to stop after every demo and build an explicit ledger before another action: input/output dimensions, separator/region counts, intersection values, compact output cells, and candidate correspondence. A demo request must be driven by named competing mappings and a specific predicted distinction that would change the test answer. Similar-looking partitioned grids and task IDs are not validation; exact replay across all demos is required before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-3/play/users/u014/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-f0caf098-request-and-inference-review.md"}

